Production Deployment Guide: Self-Hosted LLM on Windows & WSL2
1. Objective

This document outlines the complete, end-to-end procedure for deploying a self-hosted LLM web application on a Windows machine. The architecture uses Ollama on the Windows host and containerizes the Tika parsing server and Streamlit web front-end within a WSL2 Ubuntu instance. The final application is securely exposed to the internet via a Cloudflare Tunnel.

This guide is specifically designed to be robust, addressing known networking issues between modern WSL2 and Docker to ensure a stable, persistent, and automated setup.

2. Final Architecture

Windows Host: Runs the Ollama service, making LLMs available on the local network.

WSL2 (Ubuntu 24.04): Hosts all supporting services within Docker containers for portability and isolation.

Docker Engine: Manages all containerized services.

Tika Container: Provides document parsing. Managed by a systemd service for auto-start.

Streamlit Container: The web application front-end. Managed by docker-compose.

Cloudflared Container: The Cloudflare Tunnel client. Managed by a systemd service for auto-start.

Cloudflare: Provides DNS and the secure tunnel, exposing the Streamlit app to the public internet without opening any firewall ports.

3. Windows Host Preparation
3.1. Install WSL2

On a fresh Windows Enterprise build, install WSL2 from the Microsoft Store. This is the modern, recommended method.

Open PowerShell as an Administrator.

Run wsl --install -d Ubuntu-24.04.

After installation, reboot your computer.

Open the "Ubuntu 24.04 LTS" app from the Start Menu to complete the initial setup (create a user and password).

3.2. Install Ollama

Download and install Ollama for Windows from the official website.

Open a Command Prompt and pull your desired model (e.g., ollama pull g27).

3.3. Configure Windows Firewall

Create a firewall rule to allow WSL to access Ollama on the Windows host.

Open "Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security".

Click "Inbound Rules" -> "New Rule...".

Select Port and click Next.

Select TCP and specify port 11434. Click Next.

Select Allow the connection and click Next.

Ensure only the Private profile is checked. Uncheck Public. Click Next.

Give the rule a descriptive name (e.g., "Allow WSL to Ollama") and click Finish.

